    (v_topic_id, 'A 65-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the tibia. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass", "Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Osteoblast overactivation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 56-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal radius. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 30-year-old patient presents with a slowly growing, deep, painless mass in the popliteal fossa. MRI shows a heterogeneous mass near the knee joint, but not within the joint capsule. Biopsy confirms a soft tissue sarcoma. Genetic testing reveals a t(X;18) chromosomal translocation. What is the diagnosis?', '["Rhabdomyosarcoma", "Clear cell sarcoma", "Synovial Sarcoma", "Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma", "Liposarcoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Despite its name, Synovial Sarcoma rarely arises from the actual synovium of the joint, but typically occurs near large joints (e.g., the knee) in young adults. It is strongly associated with the t(X;18) translocation, which creates the SS18-SSX fusion gene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 22-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the radius that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Cryoablation without imaging", "Radiation therapy", "Open en bloc resection", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Intra-articular steroid injection"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the femur. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Osteoblast overactivation", "Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 16-year-old male presents with deep, aching pain in the posterior spine that is characteristically worse at night. The pain is completely relieved by ibuprofen. Radiographs show a small (< 1.5 cm) radiolucent nidus surrounded by dense reactive sclerosis. What is the most appropriate definitive management if symptoms become refractory?', '["Radiation therapy", "Wide surgical resection", "Intravenous bisphosphonates", "Radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Curettage and bone grafting"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Osteoid osteoma classically presents with night pain relieved by NSAIDs due to high prostaglandin (PGE2) production by the tumor nidus. CT is the best imaging modality to visualize the nidus. If medical management fails or is poorly tolerated, percutaneous radiofrequency ablation (RFA) is the treatment of choice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 14-year-old patient is diagnosed with high-grade conventional osteosarcoma of the distal femur. They are scheduled for a limb-salvage procedure. According to standard protocol, what must precede the surgical resection?', '["Denosumab", "Amputation", "Radiation therapy", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy", "Bisphosphonates"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Standard treatment for high-grade osteosarcoma includes neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, which targets micrometastases and shrinks the tumor, followed by wide surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old male presents with deep, aching pain in the tibial diaphysis that is characteristically worse at night. The pain is completely relieved by ibuprofen. Radiographs show a small (< 1.5 cm) radiolucent nidus surrounded by dense reactive sclerosis. What is the most appropriate definitive management if symptoms become refractory?', '["Chemotherapy followed by limb-salvage surgery", "Radiation therapy", "Curettage and bone grafting", "Radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Wide surgical resection"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Osteoid osteoma classically presents with night pain relieved by NSAIDs due to high prostaglandin (PGE2) production by the tumor nidus. CT is the best imaging modality to visualize the nidus. If medical management fails or is poorly tolerated, percutaneous radiofrequency ablation (RFA) is the treatment of choice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 29-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the humerus that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Open en bloc resection", "Intra-articular steroid injection", "Radiation therapy", "Cryoablation without imaging", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal radius. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 65-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal radius. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal humerus. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 14-year-old patient presents with rapid swelling of the radius. Radiographs show an eccentric, expansile metaphyseal lesion. MRI shows multiple fluid-fluid levels. What does this MRI finding represent?', '["Separation of cellular blood components from serum", "Pus settling from an abscess", "Contrast pooling in a hemangioma", "Necrotic tumor debris", "Fat floating on water within a lipoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ''''fluid-fluid level'''' on MRI in an aneurysmal bone cyst (ABC) represents the settling of red blood cells out of serum in the cyst''''s cavernous, blood-filled spaces.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 25-year-old patient presents with knee pain. Radiographs reveal an eccentric, expansile, lytic lesion resembling a ''soap bubble'' in the epiphysis of the distal femur extending to the subchondral bone. Biopsy shows mononuclear cells and numerous multinucleated giant cells. Which targeted systemic therapy can be used as an adjunct for large, unresectable, or recurrent lesions?', '["Imatinib (Tyrosine kinase inhibitor)", "Denosumab (RANKL inhibitor)", "Infliximab (TNF-alpha inhibitor)", "Rituximab (Anti-CD20)", "Methotrexate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Giant Cell Tumor of bone classically occurs in skeletally mature individuals (epiphysis/metaphysis). The neoplastic mononuclear stromal cells express RANKL, which recruits and activates the multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells. Denosumab, a monoclonal antibody against RANKL, is an effective targeted therapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old patient presents with rapid swelling of the radius. Radiographs show an eccentric, expansile metaphyseal lesion. MRI shows multiple fluid-fluid levels. What does this MRI finding represent?', '["Pus settling from an abscess", "Fat floating on water within a lipoma", "Separation of cellular blood components from serum", "Necrotic tumor debris", "Contrast pooling in a hemangioma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ''''fluid-fluid level'''' on MRI in an aneurysmal bone cyst (ABC) represents the settling of red blood cells out of serum in the cyst''''s cavernous, blood-filled spaces.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old patient presents with chronic, dull knee pain. Radiographs reveal a well-demarcated, small lytic lesion exclusively located within the epiphysis of the proximal tibia. Biopsy shows ''chicken-wire'' calcifications and mononuclear cells. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Chondroblastoma", "Enchondroma", "Chondromyxoid fibroma", "Giant Cell Tumor", "Osteosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Chondroblastoma is a rare, benign cartilage-forming tumor that characteristically occurs in the epiphysis (or apophysis) of long bones in skeletally immature patients (unlike Giant Cell Tumors, which usually occur after physeal closure). Histology classically shows ''''chicken-wire'''' calcifications.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 21-year-old presents with a painful, enlarging mass around the proximal humerus. Radiographs reveal a destructive, mixed lytic and blastic lesion in the metaphysis, with a ''sunburst'' periosteal reaction and a Codman triangle. A biopsy shows malignant cells producing unmineralized osteoid. Which of the following is the standard treatment protocol?', '["Neoadjuvant radiation and chemotherapy without surgery", "Curettage, phenol adjuvant, and cementation", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy, surgical resection, and adjuvant chemotherapy", "Primary radiation therapy followed by resection", "Surgical resection alone"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Osteosarcoma is the most common primary bone malignancy in adolescents. It typically occurs in the metaphysis of long bones. Histologic hallmark is the production of osteoid by malignant cells. Standard treatment is neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, wide surgical resection (limb salvage), followed by adjuvant chemotherapy based on tumor necrosis rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 47-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal tibia. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
