    (v_topic_id, 'A 15-year-old presents with a painful, enlarging mass around the proximal tibia. Radiographs reveal a destructive, mixed lytic and blastic lesion in the metaphysis, with a ''sunburst'' periosteal reaction and a Codman triangle. A biopsy shows malignant cells producing unmineralized osteoid. Which of the following is the standard treatment protocol?', '["Primary radiation therapy followed by resection", "Curettage, phenol adjuvant, and cementation", "Surgical resection alone", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy, surgical resection, and adjuvant chemotherapy", "Neoadjuvant radiation and chemotherapy without surgery"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Osteosarcoma is the most common primary bone malignancy in adolescents. It typically occurs in the metaphysis of long bones. Histologic hallmark is the production of osteoid by malignant cells. Standard treatment is neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, wide surgical resection (limb salvage), followed by adjuvant chemotherapy based on tumor necrosis rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 9-year-old child presents with a limp. Radiographs of the proximal femur show a ''shepherd''''s crook'' deformity with an intramedullary lesion demonstrating a ''ground-glass'' appearance. The patient also has large, irregular cafe-au-lait spots with ''coast of Maine'' borders. What other clinical feature might this patient exhibit?', '["Retinal hemangioblastomas", "Axillary freckling and Lisch nodules", "Precocious puberty", "Hearing loss and cranial nerve palsies", "Multiple lipomas"]'::jsonb, 2, 'McCune-Albright syndrome consists of the triad of polyostotic fibrous dysplasia (ground-glass bone lesions, shepherd''''s crook deformity), café-au-lait macules with irregular borders (coast of Maine), and endocrine abnormalities (most commonly precocious puberty). It is caused by an activating mutation in the GNAS gene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 18-year-old male presents with deep, aching pain in the tibial diaphysis that is characteristically worse at night. The pain is completely relieved by ibuprofen. Radiographs show a small (< 1.5 cm) radiolucent nidus surrounded by dense reactive sclerosis. What is the most appropriate definitive management if symptoms become refractory?', '["Curettage and bone grafting", "Chemotherapy followed by limb-salvage surgery", "Radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Wide surgical resection", "Radiation therapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Osteoid osteoma classically presents with night pain relieved by NSAIDs due to high prostaglandin (PGE2) production by the tumor nidus. CT is the best imaging modality to visualize the nidus. If medical management fails or is poorly tolerated, percutaneous radiofrequency ablation (RFA) is the treatment of choice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 25-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the radius that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Cryoablation without imaging", "Open en bloc resection", "Radiation therapy", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Intra-articular steroid injection"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal radius. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 58-year-old patient presents with a slowly worsening ache in the pelvis. Radiographs show a large, poorly defined intramedullary lesion with endosteal scalloping and prominent ''popcorn'' or ''ring-and-arc'' calcifications. Biopsy confirms malignant cartilage. What is the primary modality of treatment?', '["High-dose radiation therapy", "Wide surgical resection", "Radiofrequency ablation", "Denosumab therapy", "Systemic chemotherapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Chondrosarcomas are malignant tumors of cartilage typically seen in older adults (pelvis, proximal femur). They are notoriously resistant to both chemotherapy and radiation therapy. Therefore, wide surgical excision is the mainstay of treatment for conventional chondrosarcomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the femur. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass", "Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Osteoblast overactivation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 23-year-old patient presents with rapid swelling of the tibia. Radiographs show an eccentric, expansile metaphyseal lesion. MRI shows multiple fluid-fluid levels. What does this MRI finding represent?', '["Contrast pooling in a hemangioma", "Necrotic tumor debris", "Separation of cellular blood components from serum", "Pus settling from an abscess", "Fat floating on water within a lipoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ''''fluid-fluid level'''' on MRI in an aneurysmal bone cyst (ABC) represents the settling of red blood cells out of serum in the cyst''''s cavernous, blood-filled spaces.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 16-year-old patient presents with rapid swelling of the humerus. Radiographs show an eccentric, expansile metaphyseal lesion. MRI shows multiple fluid-fluid levels. What does this MRI finding represent?', '["Necrotic tumor debris", "Contrast pooling in a hemangioma", "Fat floating on water within a lipoma", "Pus settling from an abscess", "Separation of cellular blood components from serum"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The ''''fluid-fluid level'''' on MRI in an aneurysmal bone cyst (ABC) represents the settling of red blood cells out of serum in the cyst''''s cavernous, blood-filled spaces.');
